WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(group chattering)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:01.758
- [Kelly] Cheers to you.

00:00:01.758 --> 00:00:02.591
- Oh my gosh.

00:00:02.591 --> 00:00:03.746
- [Kelly] Oh, you don't like salt?

00:00:03.746 --> 00:00:04.595
I like the salt.

00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:05.785
- Okay, I kind of got into-

00:00:05.785 --> 00:00:07.501
- [Renée] Cheers.
- Cheers.

00:00:07.501 --> 00:00:08.334
Cheers.

00:00:08.334 --> 00:00:09.500
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.644
- Welcome to the show. I'm
such a fan of you both.

00:00:11.644 --> 00:00:13.216
I was so excited to-
- I got your sweet note,

00:00:13.216 --> 00:00:14.466
thank you so much.

00:00:14.466 --> 00:00:16.020
- I'm seriously, no joke, I'm a very-

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:17.359
- She wrote me a note,
I'm keeping it forever.

00:00:17.359 --> 00:00:19.052
- I know, I was being honest, too.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.972
Thank you so much for
coming, I'm so stoked.

00:00:20.972 --> 00:00:22.777
- I'm so excited to be here and meet you.

00:00:22.777 --> 00:00:23.610
(audience applause)

00:00:23.610 --> 00:00:24.443
- Have y'all known each other?

00:00:24.443 --> 00:00:25.276
- I missed you!

00:00:25.276 --> 00:00:26.109
- [Renée] I miss you, too!

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:27.751
Did you know each other
before this project?

00:00:27.751 --> 00:00:28.584
- No!

00:00:28.584 --> 00:00:29.417
- Oh, really?

00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:31.478
- We met on zoom, it was so romantic

00:00:31.478 --> 00:00:32.415
(all laugh)

00:00:32.415 --> 00:00:34.530
- [Kelly] Romantic, that's amazing.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.757
- I really felt her energy
coming through the ether.

00:00:37.757 --> 00:00:38.590
Yeah.
- Ditto.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:39.648
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:39.648 --> 00:00:41.085
- That's so awkward, it's awesome.

00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:41.918
- I know, it's so weird.

00:00:41.918 --> 00:00:43.264
And then I think the second time I met you

00:00:43.264 --> 00:00:45.275
you were fully Pam.

00:00:45.275 --> 00:00:46.108
So, it was a long time.

00:00:46.108 --> 00:00:46.941
- [Kelly] That's real different.

00:00:46.941 --> 00:00:48.154
- [Judy] It was on set.

00:00:48.154 --> 00:00:50.343
It was a long time until I got to see,

00:00:50.343 --> 00:00:51.577
I think it was like Thanksgiving.

00:00:51.577 --> 00:00:53.087
- Yeah, we properly met on Thanksgiving.

00:00:53.087 --> 00:00:53.920
- Yes, yes.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.178
- Well, you looked different too.

00:00:55.178 --> 00:00:56.086
She went full on,

00:00:56.086 --> 00:00:57.649
she did the dark hair
- Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:00:57.649 --> 00:00:59.277
and the dark contacts.
- [Kelly] Ah, yeah.

00:00:59.277 --> 00:01:00.710
- [Judy] Yeah, yeah.
I did do a little bit.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:01.643
- Do you like doing that?

00:01:01.643 --> 00:01:03.355
Getting in character like
that, and completely changing.

00:01:03.355 --> 00:01:05.687
- Oh my gosh, cheating. Like, cheating.

00:01:05.687 --> 00:01:06.987
I don't have to do anything else.

00:01:06.987 --> 00:01:07.820
I look in the mirror and I'm like,

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.246
"I'm a totally different person, great."

00:01:09.246 --> 00:01:10.079
(Renée laughs)

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:11.152
- It's so fun.

00:01:11.152 --> 00:01:13.633
It's like how they morph
you, it's such an art.

00:01:13.633 --> 00:01:16.745
- I know, it was so fun
and I never get to do that

00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:18.426
and it was really great.
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:01:18.426 --> 00:01:19.787
- And now I only wanna do that.

00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:21.048
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:21.048 --> 00:01:23.737
- So wait, explain who
you play in the series.

00:01:23.737 --> 00:01:28.370
- So, I play Leah Askey and
she is the district attorney

00:01:28.370 --> 00:01:32.109
in the county where this murder happened,

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.276
and she is persecuting Ray

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:40.133
and believes Pam to be
innocent of this crime.

00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:41.373
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:41.373 --> 00:01:44.064
- [Kelly] That was a good,
I've seen it, so that's good.

00:01:44.064 --> 00:01:45.162
- That is who I play.

00:01:45.162 --> 00:01:46.240
(Kelly and Judy laugh)

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.173
- And, there.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.332
It's a dark story.

00:01:48.332 --> 00:01:50.932
So, what do you feel like
you've learned about humanity

00:01:50.932 --> 00:01:51.765
while doing it?

00:01:51.765 --> 00:01:53.231
'Cause I was telling her, sometimes I,

00:01:53.231 --> 00:01:54.610
even hearing about this Dateline episode,

00:01:54.610 --> 00:01:57.839
I was like, humans can be horrible people.

00:01:57.839 --> 00:01:58.672
- [Judy] Yes, yes. It turns out.

00:01:58.672 --> 00:01:59.786
- Like, it scares you. Yeah.

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.201
- I know, it was...

00:02:02.201 --> 00:02:05.216
it's always strange to play someone,

00:02:05.216 --> 00:02:06.443
and I don't know if you feel this way too,

00:02:06.443 --> 00:02:09.058
that you don't agree with or
you wouldn't be friends with,

00:02:09.058 --> 00:02:11.970
or you would never make
any of their life choices.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:14.317
(all laugh)

00:02:14.317 --> 00:02:17.129
- So, I always find that, (Judy laughs)

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:21.247
I always find I need a
way in, and in this sense,

00:02:21.247 --> 00:02:23.426
I learned that most of the time,

00:02:23.426 --> 00:02:26.035
people are obsessed with being right.

00:02:26.035 --> 00:02:28.396
And that was kind of my
way into this character,

00:02:28.396 --> 00:02:29.229
and that is something-

00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:30.062
- Oh, instead of doing the right thing,

00:02:30.062 --> 00:02:31.211
they just wanna be right.
- I just like being right.

00:02:31.211 --> 00:02:32.642
Like, Leah wanted to be right.

00:02:32.642 --> 00:02:33.999
There's so many things in my life

00:02:33.999 --> 00:02:35.384
I just wanna be right about,

00:02:35.384 --> 00:02:38.583
and being right is more
important than being right,

00:02:38.583 --> 00:02:39.840
you know?
- Yeah.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:42.101
So, that was kind of
something that I worked on

00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:43.830
and learned about.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:46.853
In all of the research that we did...

00:02:46.853 --> 00:02:49.344
Well, I should say that
Dateline did for me.

00:02:49.344 --> 00:02:50.722
(Judy and Renée laugh)

00:02:50.722 --> 00:02:52.562
- Thank you, Dateline.
- Thanks, Dateline.

00:02:52.562 --> 00:02:53.914
(all laugh)

00:02:53.914 --> 00:02:57.094
- It was the wig and Dateline
and I was ready to act.

00:02:57.094 --> 00:03:00.007
But yeah, so it was like,
I learned a lot about

00:03:00.007 --> 00:03:03.725
that people sometimes just
really get set in a direction

00:03:03.725 --> 00:03:05.736
and it's really hard
to get them out of it.

00:03:05.736 --> 00:03:06.917
- Is that, I didn't even ask you that,

00:03:06.917 --> 00:03:08.072
is that weird to play a character

00:03:08.072 --> 00:03:09.690
that you might have nothing in common with

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:10.923
but you just have to dive in.

00:03:10.923 --> 00:03:13.205
- Well, you hope to, because
it's more interesting,

00:03:13.205 --> 00:03:14.296
'cause you're gonna learn something.

00:03:14.296 --> 00:03:15.856
You know, you don't wanna
go out and play somebody

00:03:15.856 --> 00:03:17.628
who's just like you (faintly speaking).

00:03:17.628 --> 00:03:20.026
- [Kelly] Loathe?
- [Judy] Boring.

00:03:20.026 --> 00:03:22.960
- But it's harder not to
judge, is the thing, you know?

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.402
Because you wanna be able to
figure out a way to empathize.

00:03:25.402 --> 00:03:27.470
Because everybody thinks that, you know,

00:03:27.470 --> 00:03:28.436
they're doing the best they can

00:03:28.436 --> 00:03:31.542
or that they're correct
in making the decision

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:32.633
that they are.

00:03:32.633 --> 00:03:33.519
They're motivated by something

00:03:33.519 --> 00:03:36.163
and they justify it in some way. But-

00:03:36.163 --> 00:03:37.074
- Seeing what makes them tick.

00:03:37.074 --> 00:03:37.907
- Yeah.

00:03:37.907 --> 00:03:39.878
But I think it is interesting
what you were talking about,

00:03:39.878 --> 00:03:42.605
because that was one of the
things that I was thinking about

00:03:42.605 --> 00:03:44.552
driving up the freeway
listening to the podcast,

00:03:44.552 --> 00:03:46.675
which is how is it that this happened?

00:03:46.675 --> 00:03:47.757
How is it that this happened,

00:03:47.757 --> 00:03:50.321
that people were able to
just dismiss what seemed,

00:03:50.321 --> 00:03:53.405
to be blatantly clear
right in front of them.

00:03:53.405 --> 00:03:57.430
In terms of the evidence and
who's guilt it points to.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:00.250
And it just says a lot about personal bias

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.974
and confirmation bias and how sure we are

00:04:02.974 --> 00:04:05.892
about our perspective based
on our own experiences

00:04:05.892 --> 00:04:08.553
and how hesitant we are to
kind of shift perspective

00:04:08.553 --> 00:04:10.472
or be challenged to change our minds

00:04:10.472 --> 00:04:12.092
when the new information comes in.

00:04:12.092 --> 00:04:12.925
- [Kelly] Mm-hmm.

00:04:12.925 --> 00:04:13.758
- And it feels like, wow,

00:04:13.758 --> 00:04:15.550
that's a really relevant topic right now,

00:04:15.550 --> 00:04:17.033
because the way that we treat each other.

00:04:17.033 --> 00:04:17.866
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:04:17.866 --> 00:04:19.856
- You know, we could
probably pause for a minute

00:04:19.856 --> 00:04:22.667
and try to better understand
somebody else's perspective

00:04:22.667 --> 00:04:24.313
based on their experiences, you know?

00:04:24.313 --> 00:04:25.146
- Absolutely.

00:04:25.146 --> 00:04:27.262
- Yeah, it just seemed
like a really interesting

00:04:27.262 --> 00:04:29.001
and relevant conversation.
- It's like that thing

00:04:29.001 --> 00:04:31.206
that you learn when you're
a kid when they were like,

00:04:31.206 --> 00:04:32.679
"put yourself in somebody else's shoes."

00:04:32.679 --> 00:04:33.620
- [Renée] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:34.834
- Even if you don't
agree with those shoes,

00:04:34.834 --> 00:04:36.329
just like, "why did you
make this decision?"

00:04:36.329 --> 00:04:37.162
- [Renée] Yeah, yeah.

00:04:37.162 --> 00:04:38.962
- There's a lot more
that you can never know

00:04:38.962 --> 00:04:40.934
about a stranger and why
they might to be doing

00:04:40.934 --> 00:04:42.348
this one thing in front of you.

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:43.489
- [Renée] Mm-hm.
- Yeah.

00:04:43.489 --> 00:04:45.994
Have you ever gotten away with anything?

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:46.891
(Judy and Renée laugh)

00:04:46.891 --> 00:04:48.427
Like, in real life?

00:04:48.427 --> 00:04:50.039
- I feel like I'm getting
away with everything.

00:04:50.039 --> 00:04:51.121
(Judy speaks faintly)
(Renée laughs)

00:04:51.121 --> 00:04:52.294
- In real life, have you ever
gotten away with anything

00:04:52.294 --> 00:04:54.183
that you're like, "oh yeah, I did it."

00:04:54.183 --> 00:04:55.016
- Yeah.

00:04:55.016 --> 00:04:56.124
- You have?

00:04:56.124 --> 00:04:56.957
Name one.

00:04:56.957 --> 00:04:58.809
- Well, I mean.

00:04:58.809 --> 00:04:59.642
Yes.

00:04:59.642 --> 00:05:02.949
When I was in high school
they built a fancy new mall

00:05:02.949 --> 00:05:06.658
by my parents' house and it
had a very fancy movie theater

00:05:06.658 --> 00:05:10.543
which had a really great back
door that would often be open

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:12.245
when people were leaving the movies.

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:14.870
And so in high school,
my friend Alfredo and I,

00:05:14.870 --> 00:05:17.370
we would camp out by the back
door of the movie theater.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:18.532
You can't do that anymore by the way,

00:05:18.532 --> 00:05:20.678
all the movie theaters now
you leave the way you come in,

00:05:20.678 --> 00:05:21.603
but back in the day-

00:05:21.603 --> 00:05:23.780
- No, I worked my first
job at a movie theater.

00:05:23.780 --> 00:05:24.780
- [Judy] Dude, right? So like-

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:27.030
- But here's the thing,
I used to let my friends.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:29.093
- [Judy] Right?
(all laugh)

00:05:29.093 --> 00:05:31.745
Through the back door, right? Yes.

00:05:31.745 --> 00:05:33.760
- That's why I was like, I knew.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:34.971
I was like, "oh, I know
where you're going."

00:05:34.971 --> 00:05:36.149
- [Judy] Yes, yes.

00:05:36.149 --> 00:05:39.109
- Of course you would, it's expensive.

00:05:39.109 --> 00:05:40.977
And they spent money at concession stands.

00:05:40.977 --> 00:05:42.698
Unless I said it was damaged.

00:05:42.698 --> 00:05:43.632
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:43.632 --> 00:05:44.998
- This popcorn's damaged.

00:05:44.998 --> 00:05:45.992
(Jude and Renée laugh)

00:05:45.992 --> 00:05:47.232
- They'd be like, "you
can keep what's damaged."

00:05:47.232 --> 00:05:49.632
We'd just be like throwing
hot tamales on the ground.

00:05:49.632 --> 00:05:50.465
Can't sell this.

00:05:50.465 --> 00:05:51.298
- It's open.

00:05:51.298 --> 00:05:52.131
- I know.

00:05:52.131 --> 00:05:53.689
Karma is [Indistinct], hope
doesn't catch up with me.

